{
  "id": "sc1",
  "type": "slide",
  "order": 1,
  "title": "Lesson 1",
  "actions": [
    {
      "id": "action_Vkvzis44",
      "type": "spotlight",
      "elementId": "text_XBev7wPH"
    },
    {
      "id": "action_PICPe53A",
      "text": "Hello, everyone. Today we will learn how realist evaluation explains why a program succeeds for some people or settings but not for others. We will build toward creating and checking a basic causal explanation.",
      "type": "speech"
    },
    {
      "id": "action_u82875_n",
      "type": "spotlight",
      "elementId": "text_5AqpVUzm"
    },
    {
      "id": "action_jH-UlzuV",
      "text": "Realist evaluation begins with a practical question: what works, for whom, in what circumstances, and why? This recognizes that programs do not automatically produce identical results across every population or setting.",
      "type": "speech"
    },
    {
      "id": "action_pbspsbMT",
      "type": "spotlight",
      "elementId": "text_BCEMJFaO"
    },
    {
      "id": "action_UceDlXRv",
      "text": "To answer that question, we use a Context–Mechanism–Outcome configuration, often shortened to CMO. Context describes the relevant circumstances, mechanism identifies the response or reasoning triggered in those circumstances, and outcome is the resulting change.",
      "type": "speech"
    },
    {
      "id": "action_PNCXDyZw",
      "type": "spotlight",
      "elementId": "text_fftQ5xwH"
    },
    {
      "id": "action_FbKe5cZY",
      "text": "A CMO configuration is a patterned explanation, not merely a list of factors. For example, after-school tutoring in a quiet room with a small group may help learners feel supported, increasing their confidence to persist and leading to more regular attendance and homework completion.",
      "type": "speech"
    },
    {
      "id": "action_6556XYUN",
      "type": "spotlight",
      "elementId": "text_Bckv93Yv"
    },
    {
      "id": "action_dMqVXHwk",
      "text": "Our learning goal is to construct and interpret a basic CMO explanation. Keep the central logic in mind: particular circumstances can activate particular responses, which then contribute to observable outcomes. Next, we will examine each of the three components more closely.",
      "type": "speech"
    }
  ],
